      "narrative": "The question hangs in the dim lamplight of the Copper Finch. You turn to Mara Venn, who has been nursing a cup of something dark at the corner of the bar.\n\nShe looks at you, one eyebrow lifted slightly. Her gaze flicks from your face to your gear, then back.\n\n\"The Ash Gate?\" She sets her cup down and leans back. Her voice is low, unhurried. \"It's shut because Old Bram is dead. Ferryman's dead, boat's rotted at the landing. No boat, no crossing. Simple as that.\"\n\nShe pauses, watching you. The silence stretches.\n\nThen she shrugs, picks her cup back up, and takes a slow drink. That's all she offers. No elaboration. No curiosity about why you're asking. She's already turned her attention to the window, to the grey light pressing through the shutters.\n\nToran Vex, the ink-stained clerk at the next table, clears his throat nervously. He's been shuffling papers, but his eyes dart toward you, then away. He looks like he wants to say something \u2014 but when Mara's gaze slides to him, he flinches and buries his face in his notes.\n\nThe tavern creaks around you. The fire pops.\n\nWhat do you do?"
    },
