      "narrative": "Mara's jaw tightens at the push. She sets her cup down with more weight than before, and for a moment you think she'll shut you down entirely. But then she exhales, long and slow, and meets your eyes.\n\n\"Old Bram sold passage. That was his job \u2014 ferryman for the Ash Gate. Had been for years.\" She gestures vaguely toward the shuttered window. \"He'd take folk through for a handful of silver. Know the right words, pay the toll, and the gate would open.\"\n\nShe pauses, her thumb tracing the rim of her cup.\n\n\"But Bram's dead. Has been for... what, three weeks now? Four?\" She shakes her head. \"Found him slumped against the gate arch one morning. No mark on him. Heart gave out, the priest said. After that, the gate just... stayed shut. No one else knew the crossing rites, and the lock was filed through soon after.\"\n\nMara shrugs, the motion carrying a finality to it.\n\n\"That's the shape of it. Bram was the gatekeeper. Now he's not. And the gate's sealed.\"\n\nShe picks up her empty cup, signals the barkeep for another."
    },
