      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warm murmur of the tavern. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp and cool, carrying the smell of wet stone and something older, like dust stirred from a long-closed room.\n\nThe street narrows as you walk. The buildings grow older, their upper stories leaning toward each other like tired old men sharing a secret. The cobbles underfoot are slick with moisture, tufts of moss pushing between the cracks.\n\nYou round a corner and see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands alone at the end of a short cul-de-sac, a cracked black arch of stone perhaps twelve feet high and eight wide. No wall flanks it \u2014 it rises from the cobbles like a door built for a room that was never finished around it. The stone is dark with age, veined with pale streaks that catch the grey light. Beyond the arch, you can see only a faint shimmer, as if the air itself has thickened into something that refuses to show what lies beyond.\n\nA figure stands near the gate. A courier in a travel-stained cloak, hood down, studying the arch with the focused attention of someone reading a map that isn't there. They haven't noticed you yet.\n\nAt the base of the gate, you spot the remnants: a rusted iron lock, its hasp hanging loose where it was filed through. And beside it, a small pile of dark, dried petals \u2014 as if someone left flowers here, days ago, and they've since withered to nothing."
    },
